---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
const ld = [{ '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.publisherName, url: SITE.domain, email: SITE.contactEmail }];
---
<Page title="About FBA Tactics" description="Who publishes FBA Tactics, what it covers, how every figure is sourced and checked, how pages are produced, and how the site is funded." kicker="About" photo="hub-about" lede="An independent reference for people who import goods into the United States and sell them on online marketplaces." jsonld={ld}>
  <h2 id="who">Who publishes the site</h2>
  <p>FBA Tactics is published by <strong>{SITE.publisherName}</strong>{SITE.publisherLocation && <>, {SITE.publisherLocation}</>}{SITE.editorName && <>, and edited by <strong>{SITE.editorName}</strong></>}. Write to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>.</p>
  {SITE.editorBio && <p>{SITE.editorBio}</p>}
  <h2>What the site covers — and why the name</h2>
  <p>The name comes from the site's start as a guide for sellers using Fulfillment by Amazon. Today it covers the two things that decide whether an imported product makes money on any marketplace: what the platform charges on each sale, and what US import rules require before the goods can be sold. That means fee tables for Amazon, Walmart, eBay, Etsy and Shopify; guides on entry, bonds, classification, origin, valuation, additional duties, drawback and foreign-trade zones; product compliance; and the accounting and tax that follow.</p>
  <h2>Where the facts come from</h2>
  <p>Only from the body that sets the rule or the fee: the Code of Federal Regulations, Federal Register notices, public laws, IRS publications, and each marketplace's own public fee pages. Every fact carries a numbered link to its source and the date it was checked. Blogs, vendors, forums and other summaries are not cited, and a figure that cannot be traced to a source a reader can open is not published. The rules are set out on <a href="/editorial-standards/">how we check facts</a>.</p>
  <h2>How pages are produced</h2>
  <p>Pages are researched and drafted with the help of AI writing tools working from the primary sources listed on each page. Every factual statement is recorded in a claims register; a separate checking pass reopens each source and compares it with the sentence it supports, and anything the source does not support is corrected or removed. Before a page goes live, a named reviewer for the publisher reads it against its sources; the reviewer's name appears under the headline. The publisher is responsible for everything published here.</p>
  <p>The site does not test or review products, software or service providers, and does not publish testimonials.</p>
  <h2>Photos and graphics</h2>
  <p>Photos are free-license images from Unsplash (Unsplash License) or Wikimedia Commons (public-domain or Creative Commons terms), credited under each photo and on the <a href="/image-credits/">image credits</a> page. They illustrate a topic; they do not show the publisher, its staff or its customers. Charts and diagrams are drawn by the site from the sourced figures.</p>
  <h2>How the site is funded</h2>
  <p>The site is intended to be funded by display advertising from Google AdSense. Advertisers do not choose, review or influence content; ads are labeled and kept out of tables, calculators, navigation and source lists. There are no sponsored posts, affiliate links or paid placements.</p>
  <h2>What this site is not</h2>
  <p>It is not legal, tax or customs advice and is not affiliated with any marketplace or government agency. See the <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>.</p>
</Page>
